This research explores the possibility of creating a voice synthesis system without relying on annotated data, using only a list of linguistically consistent words and their frequencies. Since true linguistic unit frequencies are impossible to obtain, we use transcripts from acoustic segments identified by human listeners as phonetically similar. We demonstrate that a set of these similar phonetic units can suffice as a basis for a phoneme-based voice synthesis system. Although this approach necessitates the labelling of acoustic units, it reduces the required number of labels by dividing the total number of words by the number of phonetic units. With a large enough collection of transcriptions, we can create a trainable CMU Sphinx-based voice synthesis system that can effectively learn from these examples and produce synthesised frequencies matching the observed data, ultimately allowing the system to generate synthetic speech with plausible acoustic units as its basis. A user evaluation reveals the effect of the replacement of standard phonemes with these derived units on the quality of the synthesised speech. While the transition to the acoustic unit basis shows potential, it currently results in a lower quality synthesis than a system trained using standard phonemes. Given the Mermaid tool's limitations in formatting, the following abstract attachment represents this finding: 
